cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "dss-precovery: Precovery of Known Distant Solar System Objects on Digitized Sky Survey Plates"
version: 1.0.0
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23805409
date-released: 2026-10-30
authors:
  - family-names: Stinga
    given-names: Stefano Ivan
    affiliation: Independent researcher
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-3860-3944"
    email: stefano@stinga.jp
repository-code: "https://github.com/Sting1JP/dss-precovery"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  A Python tool that looks for a known, slow-moving Solar System object on the Digitized Sky Survey scans of old
  photographic plates (POSS-I and UK Schmidt) and measures its positions. It finds the plates taken while the
  object was on them, refits each plate's astrometry to Gaia DR3, searches the JPL Horizons uncertainty region,
  rejects stars, plate flaws and sources seen on plates from other dates (POSS-II included), and
  fits blended neighbors jointly. It includes an offline self-test, three known-answer tests (Quaoar and Eris)
  with their results, and D. Hill's POSS-I plate dates and clock corrections (CC BY 4.0).
keywords:
  - precovery
  - astrometry
  - Digitized Sky Survey
  - POSS-I
  - UK Schmidt
  - photographic plates
  - trans-Neptunian objects
  - Gaia DR3
  - JPL Horizons
